cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use these files, cite cloth-fit's garment-data they come from and the paper it accompanies.
title: FoxGirl body, skeleton and LCL skirt, the Gate 8 fixtures (from cloth-fit's garment-data)
abstract: >-
  The files Gate 8 uses as its infer and rig fixtures (until infer.elf, Cuts
  4b and 7, replaces them) and, with --allow-fixture=curvenet or
  --force-fixture=curvenet, as the garment: from V-Sekai-fire/cloth-fit's
  garment-data (vendored in this repo as vendor/cloth-fit by Cut 6):
  assets/avatars/FoxGirl/avatar.obj -> avatar.obj,
  assets/avatars/FoxGirl/skeleton.obj -> skeleton.obj,
  assets/garments/LCL_Skirt_DressEvening_003/garment.obj -> garment.obj,
  assets/garments/LCL_Skirt_DressEvening_003/skeleton.obj -> garment_skeleton.obj,
  assets/garments/LCL_Skirt_DressEvening_003/no-fit.txt -> no-fit.txt (unchanged).
  Local adaptations, all made by tools/fixtures/foxgirl_convert.py: Blender's
  frame (+Z up) becomes Godot's, (x, y, z) -> (x, z, -y); every file is
  scaled by 0.283917636 so the avatar is 1.7 m tall, then shifted by
  y -= 0.281784847 so its lowest vertex is on y = 0 (one map for all five,
  so the skirt stays consistent with its own skeleton); only v, f (position
  index only) and l records are kept. fit_config.json is cut-6's
  tools/native/foxgirl_oracle.json (the upstream foxgirl_skirt setup) with
  the *_path keys removed. The skeletons are cloth-fit's 15-joint layout, which
  util/skeleton15.gd maps any rig onto (identity for these two).
  The .gdignore beside this directory keeps Godot's OBJ importer off these
  files (it refuses the line-only skeletons); they are read as text by
  util/obj_io.gd.
authors:
